                <pb facs="0429" n="395" sameAs="frus:frus1952-54v15p1/pg395"/>


                <head><hi rendition="#italic">New Version of Alternative B</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">top secret</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1952-07-09">July 9, 1952</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>The UNC would accept in principle all POWs on conclusion of armistice could
                    return to their homes; those who elected otherwise would be brought to Panmunjom
                    in small groups. Upon arrival at Panmunjom each group would be transferred to
                    the control of the Neutral Nations Supervisory Commission. The detaining side
                    would, upon the request of the Neutral Nations Supervisory Commission, provide
                    for the logistic support, transportation and such other assistance as requested
                    by the Commission. Representatives of Red Cross Societies from both sides would
                    be given full access to all such prisoners and would be permitted to assist
                    prisoners in any way approved by the Neutral Nations Supervisory Commission.
                    Under the observation of the Neutral Nations Supervisory Commission and Red
                    Cross Societies of both sides each prisoner would be interviewed by personnel
                    selected by the Government of India. Both sides would agree to accept as binding
                    the views of each POW as expressed in these interviews—that is, if it is
                    determined by the interviewer that the personnel will accept repatriation he
                    will proceed immediately and directly to the other side and if it is determined
                    that he will not accept repatriation he will be returned immediately and
                    directly to the custody of the detaining side.</p>
            </div>
